<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olaf Guthfrithson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 941</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olaf Guthfrithson was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> from 934 and of York from 939 until his death in 941. In 937 he led the Dublin Norse in alliance with the Scots and the Britons of Strathclyde against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunanburh">Battle of Brunanburh</a>. After Æthelstan's death he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and the Five Boroughs, but he died soon afterwards.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Olaf, called Amlaíb mac Gofraid in Old Irish and Ánláf in Old English, was a son of Gofraid ua Ímair, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> from 920 to 934, who had briefly ruled Northumbria in 927. Through Gofraid he was a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Ímar</a>, founder of the Uí Ímair dynasty and one of the earliest kings of Dublin, possibly the same man as Ivar the Boneless. His brothers included Blácaire, who later ruled Dublin, and Ragnall, who ruled Northumbria in 943 and 944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Olaf first appears for certain on 10 November 933, plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>. Allied with Matudán mac Áeda, overking of Ulaid, he raided as far as Sliabh Beagh, where Muirchertach mac Néill of Ailech defeated them and they lost 240 men. After succeeding his father he raided Lagore crannog in County Meath and the next week looted the burial chamber at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knowth">Knowth</a>. In 937 he broke the boats of the Limerick Vikings on Lough Ree and captured their king, Amlaíb Cenncairech, a victory for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> over its rival settlement in what is considered the high point of Viking influence in Ireland.</p><p>The same year he crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-scotland">Constantine II of Scotland</a>, whose kingdom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> had invaded in 934, to win back the Northumbria his father had ruled. Æthelstan and his brother Edmund defeated them at Brunanburh, a site now generally identified as Bromborough in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>. Back in Ireland in 938, Olaf raided Kilcullen and is said to have taken a thousand prisoners. Soon after Æthelstan's death in October 939 he made himself king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and, according to Symeon of Durham, met King Edmund at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> to divide England between them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 941, according to the Chronicle of Melrose, Olaf raided the old Anglian church at Tyninghame in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>, perhaps to secure the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. He died that year and was succeeded in Northumbria by his cousin Olaf Cuaran. The Irish annals called him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and king of the Fair Foreigners and the Dark Foreigners. A high-status skeleton found in 2005 at Auldhame, near Tyninghame, has been suggested as his; Alex Woolf thought this could not be proved but that the man very likely died in Olaf's attacks there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olaf Guthfrithson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aethelstan seized York in 927, but Norse kings such as <b>Olaf Guthfrithson</b>, Amlaib Cuaran and Eric Bloodaxe returned repeatedly.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-york">Kingdom of York</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.95,-1.08&amp;year=931" aria-label="York on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4526 312 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Olaf Guthfrithson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 582h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4976 582h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.95,-1.08&amp;year=931">What was here in 931 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
